Z||000000||Spektrum-87GT||11-14-2001||08:21 PM||||well... my cars almost done, they told me it took longer to align the fiero right because it takes a lot more to align... something about taking out ball bearings? or some kind of bearing... they said it takes more work to align a fiero correctly and its just a plain wierd process. I'm just glad they're doing it properly... they have all the latest neat alignment gadgets  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||11-15-2001||02:52 AM||||They don't know what the F*&$ they're doing.<BR>Take it somewhere else.<P>Fieros are very easy to align.<P>Hell, you can get the car 9/10th of the way there with a floor jack, ratchet, and a tape measure.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-15-2001||07:41 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>They don't know what the F*&$ they're doing.<BR>Take it somewhere else.<P>Fieros are very easy to align.<P>Hell, you can get the car 9/10th of the way there with a floor jack, ratchet, and a tape measure.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hi Ben, <BR>Is there any pages around that show home fiero alignment? <P>thanks<BR><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 11-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||11-15-2001||08:21 AM||||Fiero alignments. 84-87 are not the simplest. but that is mainly because we are now so spoiled with easy fwd alignments. To adjust any more than frt toe, you need to losen upper ball joint(s) and move in or out for camber, and for caster you can rotate upper ball joint 180deg. (only two positions for caster) <P>And a tape measure for and alignment? not on my car thank you.  But to each there own. <P>to adjust the rear is just camber between knuckle and bottom of strut and toe with there tie rod.||||reg||1
Z||000004||shark93726||11-15-2001||11:07 AM||||You can read how to check your alignment on my web page at <A HREF="http://www.8shark.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.8shark.com/</A>  <P>Look on the alignment page under the camber, caster and toe headings.<P>Front camber on '84-'87 Fieros can be adjusted by reversing the top ball joint.<P>Caster requires removing the long bolt through the inner top a-arm, and shifting the washers to different positions.<P>These first two are crude adjustments, there is no oem provision for fine adjustment.<P>Rear camber is set by loosening the two large bolts that hold the strut to the spindle assembly, tilting the spindle toi the right angle and re-tightening.<P>All four wheels are set for toe by standard tie-rod adjustments.<P>Hope this helps ya.<P>Gerald Storvik (8shark.com) ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Butter||11-15-2001||01:06 PM||||Here is what the Pontiac GM Fiero service manual says on the subject if I can get it to post.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AlineP1_1.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AlineP2.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AlineP3.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AlineP4.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AlineP5.jpg">  <P>isthiswhereiputausername,<BR>This is where I got some of my info to do my home alinement but some of the things mentioned on shark93726's web page I know to be true.  If modifications have been made to the suspension some tweaking will need to be done to compensate.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 11-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||11-15-2001||03:42 PM||||    <BR>Fiero alignments are slightly different from most other cars. <BR>In the front end, the toe is adjusted the same as any other car. The caster is adjusted by adding or removing washers that place the fore and aft positioning of the upper control arm in its pivot bolt, thus moving the fore and aft position of the upper ball joint. The camber is non-adjustable, but sometimes they can drill out the rivets tha hold it in place and rotate it 180 degrees, all at an extra cost to you (cheapest I saw was $20 a side ten years ago). The best way to make the front camber right is to replace the upper balljoints with Moog Problem Solvers, which have a long slot where the bolts go, which gives plenty of adjustability to the camber, even if you lower it. Most cars with short/long arm type front ends use shims on the upper control arm pivot rod to move the entire control arm in or out, but the 84-87 Fieros (and Chevettes) don't have this ability (even though the alignment machine may tell the "technician" to do so). The 88's do have this ability. The good news is that the Moog Problem Solvers are cheap if you shop around, but the shop probably won't install them unless you buy them there, usually at a much higher cost than you can get them yourself. Mine cost me $20 a piece back in '95, while a certain "Fiero specialty store" wanted more than double that. All you need to install them are a drill, a jack and stands, a torque wrench and hand tools, and a pickle fork. About an hour per side if you have any clue about tools. Alignment shops hate them because they mean more work than just setting the toe for their $60 alignment price (the front wheels have to come off to make any camber adjustments), so they may try to flat-rate the cost by the hour.<BR>The rear end is really easy. The caster isn't adjusted, the camber is easy with aftermarket struts, since the lower bolt hole in the strut is slotted to allow the knuckle to be pivoted in or out on the strut. There are also camber kits (special bolts) that are reasonably priced to allow the same thing. Never needed them myself, but I hear they are convenient. Toe is set the same as the front end.<BR>||||reg||1
